  <article class="prose-3030 max-w-3xl">
    <section id="purpose" class="scroll-mt-24">
      <p class="eyebrow">purpose</p>
      <h2>Tools should help people act on their ideas.</h2>
      <p>We build affordable, privacy-first, buy-once tools and use them in real workflows and artifacts. The point is not software for its own sake. The point is helping someone become more capable, creative, and independent.</p>
      <p>That means reducing repetitive work without hiding the process, keeping ownership with the person doing the work, and showing the judgment that still matters after automation.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="name" class="scroll-mt-24">
      <p class="eyebrow mt-14">meaning_of_the_name</p>
      <h2>Thirty ideas. Thirty years. Work with a longer horizon.</h2>
      <p>3thousand30 is a reminder to look beyond quick wins: build for long-term usefulness, create for human growth rather than convenience alone, and contribute to work that could still matter decades from now.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="beliefs" class="scroll-mt-24">
      <p class="eyebrow mt-14">what_we_stand_for</p>
      <h2>Eight operating beliefs</h2>
      <div class="mt-7 grid gap-4">
        <div class="panel p-6"><h3>1. Empowerment over extraction</h3><p>A product should give the user leverage, not turn attention, data, or dependence into the real business model.</p></div>
        <div class="panel p-6"><h3>2. Ownership over dependency</h3><p>Buy-once pricing and portable local files let the workflow continue even when a subscription would otherwise become a gate.</p></div>
        <div class="panel p-6"><h3>3. Privacy as a principle</h3><p>If a tool can work locally, it should. If something must leave the machine, the user should know what, where, and why.</p></div>
        <div class="panel p-6"><h3>4. Transparency and choice</h3><p>AI-enabled apps let users choose providers, models, and cost profiles instead of hiding those decisions behind one opaque service.</p></div>
        <div class="panel p-6"><h3>5. Quality depends on judgment</h3><p>Batch generation creates options. Human review, rejection, correction, and selection create worthwhile output.</p></div>
        <div class="panel p-6"><h3>6. Capability building</h3><p>The best workflow leaves the person better able to repeat, adapt, and understand the work next time.</p></div>
        <div class="panel p-6"><h3>7. English as a working language</h3><p>We use one practical operating language while building translation tools and workflows that help work reach other audiences.</p></div>
        <div class="panel p-6"><h3>8. Organic growth over heavy advertising</h3><p>Useful products, honest use cases, and finished artifacts should do more of the convincing than manufactured urgency.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="principles" class="scroll-mt-24">
      <p class="eyebrow mt-14">product_principles</p>
      <h2>What every product should try to preserve</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Reduce dependency rather than increase it.</li>
        <li>Stay simple enough for ordinary people to begin.</li>
        <li>Help users keep ownership of their work.</li>
        <li>Respect privacy by default and avoid unnecessary cloud reliance.</li>
        <li>Make any external data flow obvious.</li>
        <li>Support provider and workflow choice whenever practical.</li>
        <li>Stay accessible in price and useful without subscription lock-in.</li>
        <li>Treat free product updates as the normal relationship, not an upsell trap.</li>
        <li>Help the user become more capable over time.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="position" class="scroll-mt-24">
      <p class="eyebrow mt-14">where_we_stand</p>
      <h2>Small tools can still make a serious difference.</h2>
      <p>We are not trying to build an everything-platform or trap every workflow inside one account. We are building focused products, practical systems, and real artifacts for people who value ownership, privacy, affordability, and judgment.</p>
      <div class="panel-green mt-8 p-7"><p class="m-0 text-lg text-retro-green">Own the tools. Understand the workflow. Keep the work.</p></div>
    </section>
  </article>
